AUDIO: 'This might be the best wall mounting solution out there for tech, especially if you\'re a photographer. So, I had these two separate ideas and they ended up coming together and meshing perfectly. The first idea was that'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the click release on the bottom of my camera seems to be only ever used to connect my camera to a tripod, but it\'s always there on the bottom of my camera.'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I wanted to build out extra accessories to give this more utility. I should be able to use this for more stuff than just connecting my camera to a tripod.'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The other idea that I had was updating the pegboard because I like the idea of a pegboard, but in practice, I just kind of end up hating it because it\'s too generic. There\'s these generic hooks'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that are fiddly. They don\'t hold anything well and it\'s just not a fun thing to use and I don\'t even really like how it looks. And those two ideas came together to create something'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely new, which I\'m calling the slide release panel. This is the updated modern version of how to connect stuff, specifically tech accessories on your wall. The slide release panel is a wall'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mounting solution for quick release plates. So, a standard 38 mm quick release can just slide right into this. It doesn\'t have any latches or anything that hold it in place because it just'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slides in and then gravity is going to hold it in place, which is why I\'m calling it a slide release rather than a quick release. The standard panel has six slots, but these are designed to mount next to each other, so you can'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have multiple of them or you can stack them on top of each other and build out the panel as big as you want. Now, my camera can go from the tripod to being mounted and stored in less than a second. Sometimes camera lenses will'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have quick release plates on the bottom, so of course that\'s going on the panel. Literally anything that has a quarter 20 thread can then have a quick release plate connected to it. So, quick release'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plates going on the bottom of my lights, my lights are going on the panel. I\'ve got this tripod adapter that goes from quick release plate on one side to MagSafe on the other side.'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s for sure going on here. And now my iPhone on the panel. This is available right now over on my store. If you want to'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pick up some of these to use in your space, the link is in the description below. It\'s already really versatile as it is, but I\'ve also started designing some accessories to expand the utility'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even more like this cold shoe mount adapter. This slots in and now you have a cold shoe mount. You can hook anything on there that has a cold shoe mount. Lots of camera accessories do like little shotgun mics'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like this. So, now those are going on the panel as well. If you\'ve watched any of my other videos, you\'ve probably seen a top-down shot like this. As a full-time YouTube'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creator, I\'m setting up top-down shots like that every single day and I used to do it with a C-stand like this. I\'d spend like 5 minutes setting this up throw a sandbag on it so it doesn\'t go anywhere, put an extension rod at the'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'top so that I could get out to where I wanted the camera to be, put some adapter on there so I can mount the camera on that and it took a long time to set up. Plus, it would like wiggle around. The camera would be jiggling all over the place. It wasn\'t ideal.'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the slide release panel began to offer a solution that eventually became much, much better. At first, I just took my camera'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and mounted it on there like that, but this setup was too close to the wall. So, the next thing I designed was a top-down extension mount. This has multiple mounting points on each side, a'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mounting point on the end. It slots onto the slide release panel just like everything else and then your camera slides onto that and you\'ve got yourself a top-down camera rig just like'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that. Then when you\'re done with it camera comes off and then the extension mount also has an additional mounting point on the side here, so it can store on the slide release panel flat against'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the wall where it\'s not taking up space when you\'re not using it. If you do purchase any of these, a couple things to be aware of. It\'s only going to be as strong as whatever you use to hook it to the wall and it\'s only going to be as'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'strong as the wall, so do not just hook it to drywall. Make sure you use a stud finder, find the wood in the wall and then use screws that are long enough to go through the drywall and into that wood to make sure it\'s securely mounted.'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Also, the screws you\'re using need to be flush against the front surface of the slide release panel since the quick release plate slides in in front of those screws. You don\'t want them to be'

